diagnostic_evidence.quote:
If $T_1 \ne T_2$, then the uniqueness of prime factorizations implies that 
\begin{align*}
    \frac{n_{T_1}}{N}=\sum_{t \in T_1} \frac{1}{p_t} = \frac{\sum\limits_{t \in T_1}\prod\limits_{t'\in T_1\setminus\set{t}}t'}{\prod\limits_{t\in T_1}p_t}
    && \text{and }&&
    \frac{n_{T_2}}{N}=\sum_{t \in T_2} \frac{1}{p_t} = \frac{\sum\limits_{t \in T_2}\prod\limits_{t'\in T_2\setminus\set{t}}t'}{\prod\limits_{t\in T_2}p_t} \ ,
\end{align*}
are fractions in lowest terms with different denominators, so $n_{T_1} \ne n_{T_2}$.
